<DocsLayout
  activeSlug="lookout-config"
>
  <article>
    <h1>Editing config in lookout</h1>
    <ReferencePills slug="lookout-config" />
    <p class="lede">
      Four panes write rather than read: the shepherd's own settings,
      secrets, any Flockfile field on a running sheep, and a dog's own
      schema. All four are reached from the dashboard, and all four file a
      change set rather than writing as you type.
    </p>

    <ShortVersion>
      <p>
        <code>s</code> opens settings, <code>S</code> secrets, and{" "}
        <code>e</code> a sheep's config. Two keystrokes change a field,
        never one, and <code>h</code> lists every key without leaving the
        screen.
      </p>
    </ShortVersion>

    <h2 id="settings">Settings</h2>
    <p>
      <code>s</code> opens a full-screen settings view over{" "}
      <code>shep.toml</code>: the four daemon fields, the whistle gate, the
      style level, and the dogs table's own enable/disable, each with a
      SOURCE column and a note on what it takes to apply. <code>s</code>{" "}
      or <code>Esc</code> closes it again, back to the flock table.
    </p>
    <p>
      Reading this screen needs nothing, gate open or closed, the same as
      everything else on the dashboard. Changing a value is a
      different story: every edit and the dog toggle need the same gate the
      action keys do, and refuse the same way if it's closed.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="120×30, a fresh $SHEP_HOME">{settingsFresh}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      SOURCE reads <code>the default</code> when <code>shep.toml</code>{" "}
      doesn't mention the field at all, and the VALUE column shows the
      compiled fallback instead of anything from disk. A fresh{" "}
      <code>$SHEP_HOME</code> ships a <code>shep.toml</code> holding only{" "}
      <code>[interpreters]</code>, so this is the state you land in the
      first time you open the screen: every field on it is a guess about
      what the daemon assumes, not a record of what anyone wrote. Once a
      field is set, SOURCE switches to <code>shep.toml</code> and VALUE
      shows what's actually there.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="120×30, some fields set">{settingsDogs}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      The <code>[dogs]</code> table is the one place SOURCE isn't the whole
      story: IN FILE says whether <code>[daemon] enabled_dogs</code> names
      the dog, SOURCE says whether it's built in or adopted from a path, and
      RUNNING joins both against the live flock. The three can disagree, and
      when they do that's the point of showing all three: <code>otel</code>{" "}
      above is running with nothing enabling it, <code>ledger</code> is
      enabled and not running, and <code>bark</code> is enabled, running,
      and still <code>silent</code> because it never finished a handshake.{" "}
      <code>space</code> arms a toggle on the selected dog and <code>Enter</code>{" "}
      sends it; unlike a scalar edit, this reaches the shepherd directly and
      needs no reload.
    </p>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      A <code>[daemon]</code> edit only ever changes <code>shep.toml</code>.
      The shepherd itself can be started with <code>SHEP_LOG_LEVEL</code>,{" "}
      <code>SHEP_SOCKET</code>, or the matching <code>--log-level</code> /
      <code>--socket</code> flags, and shep's own precedence puts those above
      the file. Lookout has no way to see what the shepherd was booted with,
      so setting <code>log_level</code> here and running{" "}
      <code>shep daemon reload</code> can do nothing at all if the process
      was started with <code>SHEP_LOG_LEVEL</code> set, and nothing on
      screen will say so. <code>[style]</code> is not an exception:{" "}
      <code>--style</code> and <code>$SHEP_STYLE</code> outrank the file
      there too. The difference is that those layers belong to lookout's own
      process, so lookout can see them. SOURCE names the one in force, the
      last column reads <code>written, but outranked</code> instead of
      promising anything, and the confirm names the layer that will keep
      winning.
    </Callout>
    <p>
      The confirm names exactly this risk when it's <code>[daemon]</code>{" "}
      that's armed:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="180×30, armed">{settingsConfirm}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      <code>log_level</code>, <code>log_json</code>, and{" "}
      <code>allow_control</code> cycle through a fixed set of values, and the
      cycle only ever moves a field from <code>the default</code> to{" "}
      <code>shep.toml</code>, never back: <code>space</code> proposes the
      next value, it's never "unset". <code>socket</code> and{" "}
      <code>max_cron_sleep</code> are the only two fields this screen can put
      back to the default, because they're free text, and clearing the
      editor back to an empty line is what unsets them. <code>style level</code>{" "}
      is optional too, but <code>shep style</code> is what owns it and can't
      clear it either, so this screen doesn't offer to.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keys, all of them local to this screen while it's open:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>s</code> opens the screen, and closes it again</li>
      <li><code>j</code>/<code>k</code>, arrows move the cursor one row</li>
      <li><code>g</code>/<code>G</code> jump to the first or last row</li>
      <li>
        <code>space</code> arms the cursor's row; pressing it again on the
        same field re-arms with the next candidate
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>Enter</code> opens the text editor on <code>socket</code>/
        <code>max_cron_sleep</code>, or sends an armed confirm
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>Esc</code> cancels an armed confirm, else abandons the text
        editor, else closes the screen; it never quits lookout
      </li>
      <li><code>r</code> re-reads <code>shep.toml</code> from disk</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="fine">
      An armed candidate is cancelled, not acted on, by any movement key, by{" "}
      <code>r</code>, or by <code>s</code>: the same rule the dashboard's own
      action keys follow, where a stray keystroke right after arming backs
      out of the question instead of both dismissing it and doing something
      else. The confirm itself renders in the status bar, the same fixed row
      the dashboard's <code>x</code>/<code>R</code>/<code>L</code> confirm
      uses.
    </p>

    <h2 id="secrets">Secrets</h2>
    <p>
      <code>S</code>, <code>s</code>'s neighbour, opens a full-screen view
      over the store <a href="/docs/secrets">the secrets page</a> covers:
      the operator's own keys from <code>secrets.json</code> and each
      provider's from <code>secrets-cache.json</code>, one tab per
      environment. <code>Esc</code> or <code>S</code> again closes it.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock label="160×48">{secretsFrame}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      Two gates, and neither implies the other. Revealing a value needs{" "}
      <code>[secrets] allow_read = true</code> in <code>shep.toml</code>,
      off by default; changing anything (setting a key, deleting one, or
      adding a new one) needs the same{" "}
      <code>lookout.allow_control</code> gate the dashboard's action keys
      do, covered above. A read-only lookout with the reveal gate on can
      look but not touch; a controlling lookout without it can rotate a
      key it can never read back.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>S</code> opens the pane, and closes it again</li>
      <li>
        <code>&larr;</code>/<code>&rarr;</code>, or <code>Tab</code>/{" "}
        <code>Shift-Tab</code>, move the environment tab
      </li>
      <li><code>j</code>/<code>k</code>, <code>g</code>/<code>G</code> move the cursor</li>
      <li><code>z</code> collapses the group under the cursor, hiding its members</li>
      <li>
        <code>v</code> reveals the selected row's value for ten seconds,
        gated on <code>allow_read</code>
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>Enter</code> opens a value input on an operator row, or
        confirms an armed delete
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>D</code> arms deleting the selected key; like every other
        armed key in lookout, it expires after ten seconds unanswered,{" "}
        <code>Enter</code> is what actually confirms it, and any other key
        cancels it
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>y</code> sends an already-revealed value to the terminal over
        OSC 52
      </li>
      <li><code>r</code> reloads the store</li>
      <li><code>q</code> quits lookout</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="fine">
      A reveal clears on far more than its own timeout: moving the
      selection, changing tabs, pressing <code>Esc</code>, reloading with{" "}
      <code>r</code>, closing the pane, quitting, or a write landing all
      drop it early, so a value never sits on screen describing a row it
      no longer belongs to.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>y</code> only says the value was sent to the terminal, never
      that it arrived: OSC 52 has no acknowledgement, and the system
      clipboard is readable by whatever else is running on the desktop, a
      wider exposure than the screen it just left. A provider row refuses
      both <code>Enter</code> and <code>D</code> outright; it's a cache of
      what a dog pushed, not something lookout can write back.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      A row taking its value from the <code>all</code> slot refuses{" "}
      <code>D</code> on a named tab: removing that slot changes every
      environment at once, so the <code>all</code> tab is where you do it
      deliberately. A delete that finds no value to remove says so rather
      than reporting a success it did not have.
    </p>

    <h2 id="the-config-pane">The config pane</h2>
    <p>
      <code>e</code> on a selected sheep opens its config over the dashboard:
      every Flockfile field, the value in force, and what changing it would
      cost. Fields sit under eight groups: process, logging, inputs,
      restart, readiness, shutdown, watch, cron. It writes as well, behind
      the same gate. Under{" "}
      <code>--read-only</code> the first key that would file an edit refuses
      the same way <code>x</code> does; the pane stays open and unchanged. A
      row marked <code>=</code> or <code>~</code> gives its own reason
      instead, in both states, because the flag would not have helped it.
    </p>
    <p>
      No edit reaches the shepherd until the pane closes. An edit is filed
      into a change set the pane carries, not sent, and <code>Esc</code>{" "}
      writes the whole set at once when it closes the pane. <code>u</code>{" "}
      undoes the most recently filed edit, one at a time, and files nothing
      itself. <code>r</code> is not an edit: it re-reads the pane's config
      from the shepherd while the pane is still open, same as any other
      request.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      A field whose grammar accepts a bare number, such as{" "}
      <code>max_memory</code> or <code>kill_timeout</code>, shows its
      resolved value rather than the digits on disk: <code>64</code> reads
      as <code>64 B</code>, and <code>1600</code> as <code>1600ms</code>,
      because a bare <code>max_memory</code> is bytes and a bare timer is
      milliseconds, neither of which is what most operators expect. The
      grammar itself is unchanged; only what the row draws is.
    </p>
    <p>Two markers can sit in front of a key, and the pane's own key hint
      names both, so the meaning is on screen wherever the pane is:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>*</code> an operator has overridden it</li>
      <li>
        <code>!</code> it is parked until the next respawn, so the running
        child is not holding it yet
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>And one in the column before those, when the pane won't edit a field:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>=</code> shep refuses a config write to it anywhere, not just
        here. That is <code>name</code> and <code>instances</code>, and the
        cost column says <code>read-only</code> to match.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>~</code> the pane has no widget for the shape, and nothing
        more. A Flockfile writes these the usual way, so the cost column
        beside them reports a real cost rather than <code>read-only</code>.
        Two fields: <code>liveness_probe</code> and{" "}
        <code>readiness_probe</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="fine">
      Both are glyphs rather than a colour on purpose. The narrowest terminal
      the pane draws into has already dropped the cost column, and{" "}
      <code>plain</code> style has no colour to read, so a greyed-out row
      would be the only signal left in the case where it says least.
    </p>
    <p>
      The last column is the field's class, which is what a change to it
      usually costs:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>now</code> the shepherd re-reads it at the next decision</li>
      <li>
        <code>next start</code> it reaches the next process this sheep spawns
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>respawn</code> the running child holds it, so that instance has
        to be replaced
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>read-only</code> identity or flock shape rather than a runtime
        knob. That is <code>name</code> and <code>instances</code>; the count
        moves with <code>shep stock</code>, not from here.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="fine">
      Read it before you write, not after. It says what kind of field this
      is, and a particular write can land differently: <code>watch</code>{" "}
      says <code>now</code> and waits for a reload if the{" "}
      <code>cwd</code> it needs is itself still parked, and{" "}
      <code>autostart</code> says <code>next start</code> and takes effect
      immediately, because shep reads it at muster. The status bar after the
      write is the one that reports what actually happened, and the{" "}
      <code>!</code> flag on the row carries it afterwards.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>cwd</code>, <code>script</code>, <code>out_file</code> and{" "}
      <code>err_file</code> get one more line on that status bar when the
      shepherd looks at the path and it looks wrong: a directory that
      isn't there, or a script it can't find. The write still lands. A
      deploy that hasn't created the directory yet is a real config, not a
      typo, so this is a warning next to the write rather than a refusal
      of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      A tab row above the fields names all eight groups. <code>Tab</code>{" "}
      walks to the next one, and <code>1</code> through <code>8</code> jump
      straight to a group by its position in that row. Either resets the
      cursor to the group's first field.
    </p>
    <p>
      A panel to the right of a wide enough pane explains whichever field
      the cursor is on: its help text, its current value, its default, an
      example, what it accepts and refuses, and which other fields it
      interacts with. Below 90 columns there's no room for the panel, so the
      field's help text draws inline instead, in a row above the field list,
      wrapped, and still following the cursor. No key toggles it either way. The cost
      column carries every row's cost alone down there. From 90 up the panel draws and
      the cost column gives way to it, since the panel already names the
      focused field's own cost. From 160 up the pane is wide enough for
      both at once.
    </p>
    <p>
      What the keys do here that <code>h</code> cannot tell you in one
      line:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>r</code> re-reads the config and leaves the cursor where it was
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>space</code> files the other value for a bool, the next one
        for a choice, or the next suggestion for <code>kill_signal</code>{" "}
        and <code>cron_restart</code>, straight into the change set. Press it
        again to walk another step. A suggestion is not a closed list:{" "}
        <code>e</code> still types anything else.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>e</code> or <code>Enter</code> opens the editor on a text or
        number field; a second <code>e</code>/<code>Enter</code> files what
        was typed, or says why the field won't take it and leaves the editor
        open on it. Two keystrokes to change a field, never one.
        Inside the pane the two keys do exactly the same thing, so learning
        either is enough.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>e</code> or <code>Enter</code> on an array field opens a list
        sub-screen instead: <code>d</code> removes the selected element,{" "}
        <code>K</code>/<code>J</code> move it, and <code>e</code>/
        <code>Enter</code> edits it, or adds one from the{" "}
        <code>+ new</code> row. Each of those files the whole array on the
        spot; there's no second key to send it. They build on each other,
        and the rows show what you have filed rather than what the shepherd
        sent, so you can remove two elements or add several before leaving.
        <code>u</code> in here drops the array's whole entry and puts the
        shepherd's own back.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>d</code> on the field list restores the selected field to its
        default, filing that too rather than sending it. A field already at
        its default, or one shep refuses to write, files nothing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>u</code> undoes the most recently filed edit, one at a time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>Esc</code> closes whatever is innermost: a sub-screen back to
        the field list, then a question if anything
        the running child hasn't taken is outstanding, and last the pane
        itself, writing the whole change set as it closes. It never quits
        lookout.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Every filed edit is recorded as yours once it's sent: the key picks up
      the <code>*</code> marker here and in <code>shep flock</code>'s{" "}
      <code>CFG</code> column, and a later <code>shep start</code> of the
      app's Flockfile leaves it alone. Nothing else in the config moves,{" "}
      <code>instances</code> included.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      A field a write cannot reach in the running child parks until the next
      spawn, since the child was given its value at exec and holds it. If
      an edit still filed needs a respawn, or a field is already parked from
      an earlier write, <code>Esc</code> asks before it closes: restart now,
      reload, or leave it running with the edits parked. <code>L</code>{" "}
      reloads the sheep, <code>R</code> restarts it, <code>c</code> writes
      and leaves it running, and <code>Esc</code> again keeps editing and
      writes nothing. The restart or reload only fires once its write has
      landed, so it never respawns into the config it was meant to replace.
      The dialog shows up only with the gate open. <code>shep reload
      &lt;sheep&gt;</code> promotes an already-parked field from the CLI,
      any time after.
    </p>
    <p>
      A sheep's env keys sit in the field list itself, beneath whichever
      group is on screen, rather than behind their own row. No value ever
      appears there, because the shepherd never sends one: every key reads
      {" "}
      <code>(set)</code>. <code>e</code> or <code>Enter</code> on a
      key types a new value for it, and filing an empty one removes the
      key. Either on the <code>+ new</code> row takes{" "}
      <code>KEY=value</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      An env write files the same way a field does, a set as much as a
      removal, and waits with everything else for the pane to close. The
      old value is overwritten in the store as soon as the write lands, and
      this screen cannot read a value back to put it there again, so an
      overwrite loses as much as a delete does.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      env is spawn-time in every case, so nothing set here reaches the running
      child until that child is replaced.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      A group row opens one too. Every instance of a multi-instance app
      shares one stored spec, so the pane is about the app rather than about
      whichever instance the cursor was over.
    </p>

    <h2 id="the-dog-config-pane">The dog config pane</h2>
    <p>
      <code>e</code> on a dog row opens its config, over that dog's{" "}
      <code>[&lt;name&gt;]</code> section in <code>dogs.toml</code>. It
      works the same whether the row sits in the dashboard's own{" "}
      <code>Dogs</code> section or the settings screen's dogs table. It
      used to refuse there and point you at <code>s</code> for settings
      instead; now the row itself is enough. Inside the pane,{" "}
      <code>e</code> is the field list's own edit key, the same as on a
      sheep's config; only <code>Esc</code> closes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The form comes from the dog. Shep asks the binary for its schema when
      the pane opens rather than reading one it stored, because{" "}
      <code>cargo install</code> replaces a dog with nothing watching and a
      stale schema mislabels which field is a credential. A built-in dog
      answers without being spawned at all. A dog that publishes no schema
      gets no pane, and a line saying where its settings do live:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{`pydog publishes no schema; edit dogs.toml with $EDITOR`}</CodeBlock>
    <Callout variant="careful">
      Asking runs it. Opening the pane on an adopted dog spawns that binary
      with <code>--schema</code>, waits a second for an answer, then kills
      it. That is a third party's code executing because you pressed a key,
      and it is worth knowing before you browse the config of a dog you did
      not write. A built-in dog is shep's own binary and is never spawned.
    </Callout>
    <p>
      The pane is flat, with no section headers: a dog's schema carries no
      groups, and inventing some would be shep deciding how somebody else's
      config reads. The cost column is empty on every row, because shep does
      not know what a dog's field costs. The foot of the pane says so once,
      naming the dog:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock>{`shep publishes the change; bark decides what to reload`}</CodeBlock>
    <p>
      A field the dog marks as a credential reads <code>&lt;set&gt;</code>{" "}
      and never its value, and the editor on it opens empty. Typing a new one
      replaces it; nothing here can show you what it replaced. A table or a
      list gets <code>~</code>, the same glyph a sheep's{" "}
      <code>args</code> gets and for the same reason.
    </p>
    <p>
      Edits file into the same change set a sheep's pane uses, and{" "}
      <code>u</code> undoes them the same way; nothing reaches the dog's
      section until <code>Esc</code> closes the pane. However many fields
      changed, the write sends the section whole, in one request, so the
      comments and key order in <code>dogs.toml</code> survive it. Applying
      an empty value removes the key, which puts the dog back on its own
      default. Neither <code>*</code> nor <code>!</code> appears: a dog has
      no Flockfile to differ from, and <code>dogs.toml</code> is the one
      copy of its settings.
    </p>
    <p>
      The explanation panel draws here too, at the same widths, with the
      dog's own help text, current value, default and example; only the
      impact sentence is missing, since shep doesn't know what a dog's
      field costs. There's no tab row: a dog's schema carries no groups to
      move between.
    </p>
    <p class="fine">
      <code>r</code> re-reads the section and not the schema, so a keystroke
      whose job is to re-read a file never respawns somebody else's binary.
    </p>
</article>
